  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-4cb11654 Web logs overview\"** (`eff-4cb11654-web-logs-overview-92e4223d7243`)\n- Requests over time (line, by `timestamp` date histogram)\n- Requests by response code (bar, top 10 values of `response.keyword`)\n- Unique client IPs (metric, unique count of `clientip`)\n- Top requested URLs (table, top 10 `url.keyword`)\n- 5xx responses (ES|QL metric, count of `response.keyword LIKE \"5*\"`)\n\nI set the dashboard time range to the last year so the sample data is visible. It's a choice you can change.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-4cb11654 5xx spike\"** (`e9cef971-25ae-4ff3-b377-b875beff49d0`)\n- Type: Elasticsearch query (`.es-query`), consumer `stackAlerts`\n- Runs every 1m over a 5m window on `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`\n- Query matches `response.keyword` wildcard `5??`, i.e. three-digit 5xx codes\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10\n- No actions attached, so it will evaluate and show in the rule list but won't notify anyone. Say if you want a connector added.\n- Alerting health reports `ok`.\n\n**Not verified:**\n- I could not query the index directly. `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` isn't set in my shell, and unauthenticated calls return 401. I used the Kibana MCP tools for creation instead. As a result I haven't confirmed the actual response code values or that any 5xx traffic exists. The `5??` filter assumes codes are stored as three-digit strings, which is how the field is typed.\n- I haven't confirmed that the rule has run or fired yet.",
